We will use this example throughout:

public class Main {

    static int x = 100;

    static {
        System.out.println("Static block executed");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 10;
        int b = 20;

        int sum = add(a, b);

        System.out.println(sum);
    }

    public static int add(int x, int y) {
        return x + y;
    }
}


<!-- 1. WRITE CODE -->

You write Java code in a text file:

Main.java

This is called Java source code.

At this point, the file contains human-readable instructions:

int a = 10;
int b = 20;

But the CPU cannot directly understand Java syntax.

The CPU ultimately understands machine instructions, so Java source code must first be processed.

Therefore:

Main.java
    ↓
Compiler


<!-- 2. COMPILE — javac Main.java -->

You compile the program using:

javac Main.java

javac is the Java compiler.

The compiler processes your source code in several stages.

Main.java
    │
    ▼
Lexical Analysis
    │
    ▼
Syntax Analysis
    │
    ▼
Semantic Analysis
    │
    ▼
Bytecode Generation
    │
    ▼
Main.class

<!-- 2.1 Lexical Analysis -->

The compiler first reads the characters and breaks them into meaningful units called tokens.

Example:

int a = 10;

The compiler identifies:

int  → Keyword
a    → Identifier
=    → Assignment operator
10   → Integer literal
;    → Separator

So:

Characters
    ↓
Tokens

The compiler now understands the individual pieces of your program.

<!-- 2.2 Syntax Analysis -->

Next, the compiler checks whether those tokens follow the grammar rules of Java.

This is valid:

int a = 10;

This is invalid:

int = a 10;

Even though both contain recognizable tokens, the second one does not follow Java's syntax.

Conceptually:

Tokens
   ↓
Does this follow Java grammar?
   │
   ├── Yes → Continue
   │
   └── No → Syntax Error

The compiler creates an internal representation of the structure of the program, often represented conceptually as an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST).

For:

int sum = add(a, b);

conceptually:

Variable Declaration
│
├── Type → int
├── Variable → sum
│
└── Method Call
     │
     ├── Method → add
     ├── Argument → a
     └── Argument → b


<!-- 2.3 Semantic Analysis -->

Now the compiler checks whether your program makes sense according to Java rules.

For example:

int a = 10;
String name = a;

The syntax is valid, but the types are incompatible.

Similarly:

int sum = add(a, b);

The compiler checks:

Does add() exist?
        ↓
Does it accept two arguments?
        ↓
Are a and b valid arguments?
        ↓
Does add() return an int?
        ↓
Can the result be stored in sum?

<!-- Other checks include: -->

Type checking
Variable existence
Method existence
Access modifiers
Inheritance rules
Exception-related compile-time checks

If something is wrong:

Compile-time Error

No successfully compiled program is produced.


<!-- 2.4 Bytecode Generation -->

If everything is valid, the compiler generates:

Main.class

This file contains Java bytecode.

So:

Main.java
     │
     │ javac
     ▼
Main.class

Bytecode is an intermediate instruction format.

It is not Java source code and generally not native CPU machine code.

It is designed to be executed by the JVM.

You can inspect bytecode using:

javap -c Main

<!-- 3. RUN — java Main -->

Now you execute:

java Main

Remember the difference:

javac Main.java
        ↓
Compiles source code
        ↓
Creates Main.class


java Main
        ↓
Starts the Java runtime/JVM
        ↓
Executes the Main class

When you type:

java Main

the operating system starts a Java process, and the JVM begins preparing to execute your program.

The JVM does not simply send the entire .class file directly to the CPU.

First, classes must be loaded and prepared.

<!-- 4. JVM STARTS -->

The JVM is responsible for running Java bytecode.

Conceptually:

Operating System
       │
       ▼
Java Process
       │
       ▼
JVM
       │
       ├── Loads Classes
       ├── Manages Memory
       ├── Executes Bytecode
       ├── Performs JIT Compilation
       └── Performs Garbage Collection

The JVM now needs to execute your:

Main

class.

That brings us to class loading.

<!-- 5. CLASS LOADING -->

The JVM lifecycle for a class is:

Class
  │
  ▼
Loading
  │
  ▼
Linking
  │
  ├── Verification
  ├── Preparation
  └── Resolution
  │
  ▼
Initialization

<!-- 5.1 Loading -->

The Class Loader locates and reads:

Main.class

Conceptually:

Disk

Main.class
    │
    ▼
Class Loader
    │
    ▼
Class information becomes available to JVM

The JVM obtains information such as:

Class Name → Main

Methods:
    main()
    add()

Fields:
    static int x

Superclass:
    Object

Your program may also need other classes.

For:

System.out.println(sum);

Java needs classes and runtime components related to:

System
PrintStream

Classes are not necessarily all loaded at the very beginning. The JVM can load classes as they become necessary.

5.2 LINKING

After loading, the class goes through linking.

Linking
│
├── Verification
├── Preparation
└── Resolution
A. Verification

The JVM checks whether the bytecode is valid and safe to execute.

Conceptually:

Main.class
    │
    ▼
Bytecode Verifier
    │
    ├── Valid structure?
    ├── Valid instructions?
    ├── Correct type usage?
    └── Valid stack operations?
    │
    ▼
Verified Bytecode

This is important because the JVM should not blindly execute arbitrary invalid bytecode.

<!-- B. Preparation -->

The JVM prepares storage for class variables.

Example:

static int x = 100;

During preparation, the JVM prepares the class-level storage needed for this field.

Then during initialization, the actual Java initialization logic, such as assigning 100, is executed.

So conceptually:

Preparation
    ↓
Prepare storage

Initialization
    ↓
Execute initialization logic

<!-- C. Resolution -->

The bytecode contains symbolic references to classes, methods, and fields.

For example, your code refers to:

add(a, b);

and:

System.out.println(sum);

The JVM must connect these symbolic references to the appropriate runtime classes, methods, or fields.

Conceptually:

Symbolic Reference
        │
        ▼
Find the actual runtime target
        │
        ▼
Class / Method / Field

Resolution may occur when needed rather than necessarily resolving everything immediately.

<!-- 5.3 INITIALIZATION -->

After linking, the class is initialized when initialization is required.

Our example contains:

static int x = 100;

static {
    System.out.println("Static block executed");
}

The initialization logic runs in source-defined order.

Conceptually:

Class Initialization
       │
       ▼
static int x = 100
       │
       ▼
static block
       │
       ▼
"Static block executed"
       │
       ▼
main()

Therefore, the output begins with:

Static block executed

before the code inside main() runs.

Important
JVM starts
    ↓
Class preparation/loading/linking
    ↓
Class initialization
    ↓
Static fields and static blocks
    ↓
main()

So main() is the entry point for the application's main execution, but static initialization associated with the class happens before its body executes.

6. main() STARTS

Now the JVM invokes:

public static void main(String[] args)

The JVM creates an execution context for the method.

Since every Java thread has its own stack, the main thread gets a stack frame for main().

Main Thread Stack

┌─────────────────────────┐
│                         │
│      main() Frame       │
│                         │
└─────────────────────────┘

This frame contains the data needed to execute the method.

7. STACK FRAME CREATED

A simplified stack frame contains:

Stack Frame
│
├── Local Variable Array
│
├── Operand Stack
│
└── Additional frame information

These are very important for understanding JVM bytecode execution.

7.1 Local Variables

Consider:

public static void main(String[] args) {
    int a = 10;
    int b = 20;
}

Conceptually, the frame can contain slots such as:

Local Variables

Slot 0 → args
Slot 1 → a = 10
Slot 2 → b = 20

Because main() is static, it does not have:

this

For an instance method:

public void test(int x) {
}

conceptually:

Slot 0 → this
Slot 1 → x
7.2 Operand Stack

The JVM bytecode execution model uses an operand stack.

Suppose Java executes:

int a = 10;

Conceptually:

Push 10
   │
   ▼

Operand Stack

┌────┐
│ 10 │
└────┘

Then the value is stored into a local variable.

Operand Stack
       │
       ▼
Local Variable Slot

a = 10

The operand stack is then ready for the next operation.

8. METHOD CALLS

Now our program executes:

int sum = add(a, b);

The JVM must call:

add(10, 20)

First, the values are prepared for the method call.

Conceptually:

Load a → 10
Load b → 20
        │
        ▼
Call add(10, 20)
A new stack frame is created

Before calling add():

Thread Stack

┌──────────────────┐
│ main()           │ ← Currently executing
└──────────────────┘

When add() is called:

Thread Stack

┌──────────────────┐
│ add()            │ ← Currently executing
├──────────────────┤
│ main()           │
└──────────────────┘

The main() method is paused while add() executes.

Inside:

public static int add(int x, int y) {
    return x + y;
}

the new frame has its own local variables:

add() Local Variables

Slot 0 → x = 10
Slot 1 → y = 20
Performing x + y

Conceptually, the values are placed on the operand stack:

Operand Stack

┌────┐
│ 20 │ ← top
├────┤
│ 10 │
└────┘

The integer addition operation:

iadd

conceptually does:

Pop 20
Pop 10
   │
   ▼
10 + 20
   │
   ▼
Push 30

Now:

Operand Stack

┌────┐
│ 30 │
└────┘

Then the result is returned.

Return → Frame Removed

When:

return x + y;

finishes:

add() Frame
      │
      ▼
Return value = 30
      │
      ▼
add() Frame is removed
      │
      ▼
Execution returns to main()

The stack becomes:

┌──────────────────┐
│ main()           │ ← Execution continues
└──────────────────┘

The returned value is stored in:

int sum = 30;
9. EXECUTION ENGINE

The JVM's execution engine is responsible for executing bytecode.

Simplified:

Execution Engine
│
├── Interpreter
│
└── JIT Compiler
9.1 Interpreter

The interpreter executes bytecode instructions.

Conceptually:

Bytecode Instruction 1
        ↓
Execute

Bytecode Instruction 2
        ↓
Execute

Bytecode Instruction 3
        ↓
Execute

This is useful, especially when code begins executing.

But repeatedly interpreting the same frequently executed code can add overhead.

For example:

for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
    add(10, 20);
}

The same code may execute many times.

This is where JIT compilation helps.

9.2 JIT Compiler

JIT means:

Just-In-Time Compiler

The JVM can detect frequently executed code paths.

Conceptually:

Bytecode
    │
    ▼
Interpreter starts execution
    │
    ▼
Code becomes frequently executed
    │
    ▼
JIT Compiler
    │
    ▼
Native Machine Code
    │
    ▼
Optimized execution

The JIT compiler can optimize code using runtime information.

So Java execution is not simply:

Always interpret every instruction forever

Instead, the JVM can compile important/hot code into native machine code for faster execution.

10. NATIVE MACHINE CODE

The CPU cannot execute Java bytecode directly.

Ultimately, the operations being executed must be performed using instructions understood by the processor.

Conceptually:

Java Source Code
       │
       ▼
Bytecode
       │
       ▼
JVM Execution
       │
       ├── Interpreter
       │
       └── JIT Compilation
               │
               ▼
       Native Machine Instructions
               │
               ▼
              CPU

The exact native instructions depend on the processor architecture.

For example:

x86-64
ARM64

can have different machine instruction sets.

This is one reason Java bytecode helps with portability: the same Java bytecode can be executed by JVM implementations designed for different platforms.

CPU EXECUTION AND OUTPUT

Now consider:

System.out.println(sum);

The JVM executes the required method calls.

Conceptually:

System
   │
   ▼
out
   │
   ▼
PrintStream object
   │
   ▼
println(sum)
   │
   ▼
Standard Output
   │
   ▼
Operating System / Terminal
   │
   ▼
Screen

The user sees:

30

So the complete chain is:

Java statement
      ↓
Bytecode operation
      ↓
JVM execution
      ↓
Required Java library methods
      ↓
Operating system output facilities
      ↓
Terminal/Console
11. main() FINISHES

Eventually, the last statement in main() executes.

System.out.println(sum);

Then main() reaches its end.

The main() stack frame is removed.

Before:

Main Thread Stack

┌──────────────────┐
│ main()           │
└──────────────────┘

After main() returns:

Main Thread Stack

(empty)

Conceptually:

main() completes
       │
       ▼
main() stack frame removed
       │
       ▼
Main thread finishes
JVM TERMINATES

If no remaining non-daemon threads are running, the JVM can terminate.

main() finishes
       │
       ▼
Are non-daemon threads still running?
       │
   ┌───┴───┐
   │       │
  Yes      No
   │       │
   ▼       ▼
JVM keeps   JVM
running     terminates

Therefore, normally:

main() finishes
      ↓
All required non-daemon threads finish
      ↓
JVM shuts down
